#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f573cc64-d267-5858-8999-94a62c406ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341147} {:answer/id #uuid "8f365832-86e4-5baa-9516-d3de574eeab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341148} {:answer/id #uuid "25787bea-51ab-5c67-a7dd-0e667a74fbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|-\\frac{3}{x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341149} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec8c233-a8e2-5830-ae10-9e7e4a8bce28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}|+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5{x}^{2}|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341150} {:answer/id #uuid "bda5ebc0-86f0-5e79-a9f9-6b334c5cc919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}|-\\frac{1}{x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f365832-86e4-5baa-9516-d3de574eeab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341148} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd62682-e831-583e-8986-092603e9e534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b2cc1c23-ecaf-5055-8847-070b8ab85d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-3609" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by ${x}^{2}(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x-5=A(x)(x-5)+B(x-5)+C{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({5}^{2})-2(5)-5 & =A(5)(5-5)+B(5-5)+C(5)^{2} \\\\ 10 & =C(25) \\\\ C & =\\frac{10}{25} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-2(0)-5 & =A(0)(0-5)+B(0-5)+C(0)^{2} \\\\ -5 & =B(-5) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-5}{-5} \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({1}^{2})-2(1)-5 & =A(1)(1-5)+B(1-5)+C(1)^{2} \\\\ -6 & =-4A-4B+C \\\\ -6 & =-4A-4(1)+(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 4A & =6-4+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 4A & =\\frac{10+2}{5} \\\\ A & =\\frac{12}{20} \\\\ A & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}=\\frac{3}{5x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{5x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5(x-5)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{x-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad5ad403-f4a0-5a5a-80e6-ba2c928db9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-3)^{2}|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341154} {:answer/id #uuid "8a722181-452d-5391-9391-a9b7fb9b1a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{9}{x-3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341155} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c909cf-3aa4-5b59-8456-269a68640172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{9}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341156} {:answer/id #uuid "d920dbee-5399-57cc-a3f2-b636639812aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{2}{x-3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341157} {:answer/id #uuid "4208137c-1ae4-54c5-b67b-de7dc2a1db96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{9}{x-3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a722181-452d-5391-9391-a9b7fb9b1a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{9}{x-3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341155} :answer-field/id #uuid "731ab4af-fa85-5905-ab15-314657743ef8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8085c10f-3d6e-5cb9-841e-70118adc41af" :question/math-academy-id "q-3619" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{x-3}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)(x-3)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=A(x-3)^{2}+B(x-3)(x-2)+C(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{2} & =A(3-3)^{2}+B(3-3)(3-2)+C(3-2) \\\\ 9 & =C \\\\ C & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$  we can eliminate  $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2} & =A(2-3)^{2}+B(2-3)(2-2)+C(2-2) \\\\ 4 & =A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2} & =A(0-3)^{2}+B(0-3)(0-2)+C(0-2) \\\\ 0 & =9A+6B-2C \\\\ 0 & =9(4)+6B-2(9) \\\\ 0 & =18+6B \\\\ -6B & =18 \\\\ B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{3}{x-3}+\\frac{9}{(x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{3}{x-3}+\\frac{9}{(x-3)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+9\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{9}{x-3}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309434ff-006c-5570-be38-7c74c12f0b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341161} {:answer/id #uuid "e41d2c18-5d16-57f1-bb74-eaa8e6308505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341162} {:answer/id #uuid "874c003c-77de-55a5-b275-aae411bd0cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341163} {:answer/id #uuid "644eb290-ff2c-57df-b7b7-0cdf2541eef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341164} {:answer/id #uuid "d910c2f2-8a24-5a08-b366-a961b0920510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡11+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41d2c18-5d16-57f1-bb74-eaa8e6308505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341162} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc3ad62-5455-51d6-8a91-39ffda8f746b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb06addb-dbc5-584b-b45e-eea6d5515fa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49901" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}=\\frac{A}{(x-4)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3-x)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-4)^{2}(3-x)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9x+21=A(x-4)(3-x)+B(3-x)+C(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2}-9(4)+21 & =A(4-4)(3-4)+B(3-4)+C(4-4)^{2} \\\\ 16-36+21 & =B(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}-9(3)+21 & =A(3-4)(3-3)+B(3-3)+C(3-4)^{2} \\\\ 9-27+21 & =C{(-1)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{1} \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-9(0)+21 & =A(0-4)(3-0)+B(3-0)+C(0-4)^{2} \\\\ 21 & =-12A+3B+16C \\\\ 21 & =-12A+3(-1)+16(3) \\\\ 21 & =-12A+45 \\\\ 12A & =45-21 \\\\ A & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}=\\frac{2}{(x-4)}-\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(3-x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{5}^{6}(\\frac{2}{(x-4)}-\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(3-x)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{3-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|{|}_{5}^{6}+\\frac{1}{x-4}{|}_{5}^{6}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-x|{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6-4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-4|)+(\\frac{1}{6-4}-\\frac{1}{5-4})-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-6|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-5|) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7e8e8af-06f2-5666-846f-0cc16e2ced70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341168} {:answer/id #uuid "85e32b4c-4c20-5634-8c37-68e69ed9d7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341169} {:answer/id #uuid "d649e9f3-a865-516b-aa83-6eab5c732fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{4}{x+2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341170} {:answer/id #uuid "15c92157-27dd-5825-b36a-4434b38cd2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{4}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341171} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfd4e20-c970-56ce-98c9-bef2e80b611b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e32b4c-4c20-5634-8c37-68e69ed9d7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341169} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6998aba-b4b4-542e-9a64-2e9319fb2473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf7ebc59-2455-5c47-9b5b-9642d6175ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-82894" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+2)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x=A(x+2)^{2}+B(x+2)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+4(-2) & =A(-2+2)^{2}+B(-2+2)+C \\\\ -4 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+4(0) & =A(0+2)^{2}+B(0+2)+C \\\\ 0 & =4A+2B-4 \\\\ 2A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}+4(-1) & =A(-1+2)^{2}+B(-1+2)+C \\\\ -3 & =A+B-4 \\\\ A+B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the equations $2A+B=2$ and $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =1 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "995b13e7-c72d-5c9e-bbbc-c45444b09d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x-2}-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341175} {:answer/id #uuid "9e96ce49-1b42-51f8-ae65-cce289df1ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{1}{x-2}-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341176} {:answer/id #uuid "bc23dca4-796f-5b16-be5a-5e79c2aed33e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341177} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b64911-3060-5f0c-97fb-44dbc0a69603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341178} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7c0479-9af2-5bc4-890b-a0e862066f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{1}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e96ce49-1b42-51f8-ae65-cce289df1ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{1}{x-2}-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341176} :answer-field/id #uuid "bed091bf-5190-59da-86c2-96452e2159c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4034c63-29b7-549e-ae42-2e9b3ad3f649" :question/math-academy-id "q-82895" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+3=A(x-2)^{2}+B(x-2)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}-3(2)+3 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-3(1)+3 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=3$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{2}-3(3)+3 & =A(3-2)^{2}+B(3-2)+C \\\\ 3 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=0$ and $A+B=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =0+2 \\\\ 2A & =2 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nand substituting back into $A-B=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-B & =0 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{1}{x-2}-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}]}
